SRC/ls IS the source of the ls on this disk===========================================It was not, until 2026-08-24.  This file used to say so, and listed threereasons a rebuild would produce a worse ls than the one shipped.  All threehave been fixed in the tree, and the ls in CMDS/ is now built from SRC/ls bytools/rebuild.What the three were, and what was done:  1.  os9stubs.c defined its own stat() and fstat().  They hardcoded one mode      for every file and never set st_mtime, so every entry listed with      invented permissions and a 1969 date.  Both are gone; the recipe links      /dd/LIB/os9lib.l, whose stat() returns real inodes, sizes and dates.  2.  isgraph() was missing.  OS-9's K&R <ctype.h> has every classifier      except that one, and ls.c reaches it through ISGRAPH() in system.h.      tools/rebuild/shims/os9isgraph.c supplies it.  3.  filemode.c's mode_string() read Unix octal permission groups      (0700/0070/0007), which do not overlap OS-9's attribute bits      (0x01/0x02/0x04 owner, 0x08/0x10/0x20 public) at all.  It now reads the      OS-9 bits, and it includes system.h so it gets them.  And a fourth, which nobody had found: os9lib's stat() SIGN-EXTENDS the  attribute byte into st_mode.  A directory (0xbf, bit 7 set) arrives as  0xffbf and a plain file (0x1b) as 0x001b.  system.h tested the file type  through an S_IFMT of 0x0380, which matched the sign fill rather than the  type, so S_ISDIR was false for every directory on this disk and  `ls -l /dd/DOC' printed the directory instead of listing it.How the rebuild compares with the binary it replaced, over 40 option cases:39 byte-identical, and one better -- `ls -i' used to print 0 for every inodeand now prints the real ones.Everything else under SRC/ builds what CMDS/ ships, or is documented innotes/COMPILE-AUDIT.md where it does not.